{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17901","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17901","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17901","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geewook","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Geewook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjoon","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Minjoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Modern Video-LLMs Need to Listen? A Benchmark Audit and Scalable Remedy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:28:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:41:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T04:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:28:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T15:58:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T01:10:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-22T00:40:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:07:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech and audio encoders developed over years of community effort are routinely excluded from video understanding pipelines, not because they fail, but because benchmarks never required listening. We audit 10 video benchmarks and find items largely solvable from visual cues alone: a single-frame probe answers about 76% of AVQA without audio, suggesting poor measurement of audio-visual reasoning. Building on LLaVA-OneVision, we attach a speech/audio encoder and compare five compressor architectures under 25-fold token reduction (25 Hz to 1 Hz). Across 10 benchmarks, with and without filtering, audio yields clear gains on tasks requiring speech comprehension or cross-modal grounding, while vision-centric suites remain largely unaffected. Our results show that speech encoders play a larger role in video understanding than current benchmarks suggest. We will open-source our work at https://github.com/naver-ai/unimambamia-av."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17901","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:22:56Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:22:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:34:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16975","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16975","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16975","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Enzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interpretable Audio Editing Evaluation via Chain-of-Thought Difference-Commonality Reasoning with Multimodal LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T08:18:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:49:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T06:46:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automatic mean opinion score (MOS) prediction serves as a principled alternative to both subjective listening tests and objective metrics, providing scalable and consistent audio evaluation. Inspired by the LLM-as-Judge paradigm, recent multimodal large language models offer strong perceptual modeling and reasoning capabilities, enabling audio quality assessment. In this work, we address the challenging problem of audio editing evaluation and propose the first natural language-based automated evaluation framework built upon Qwen2-Audio. Two caption-based fine-tuning tasks are introduced to enhance multi-audio understanding, together with a designed Chain-of-Thought prompting strategy to encourage structured, step-by-step reasoning. Experiments show that our framework produces interpretable and logically consistent text-based evaluations, aligning closely with human judgments while outperforming existing baselines. The code and demo are available at https://github.com/NKU-HLT/Eval_Reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16975","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:01:03Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:01:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18085","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18085","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18085","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sudhanshu","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Sudhanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Risheek","familyName":"Garrepalli","name":"Garrepalli, Risheek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghavv","familyName":"Goel","name":"Goel, Raghavv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Lott","name":"Lott, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatih","familyName":"Porikli","name":"Porikli, Fatih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingu","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Mingu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structuring The Future: Diffusion LLM Speculative Decoding via Calibrated Draft Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:58:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:50:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:38:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:11:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-14T06:58:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:26:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-12T00:16:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion LLMs (dLLMs) have recently emerged as a powerful alternative to autoregressive LLMs (AR-LLMs) with the potential to operate at significantly higher token-generation rates. To unlock this potential, we present Spiffy, a speculative decoding algorithm to accelerate dLLM inference while provably preserving the model's output distribution. This work addresses the unique challenges involved in applying ideas from speculative decoding of AR-LLMs to dLLMs. Spiffy performs auto-speculation to eliminate the overheads of an independent draft model, structuring draft states in the form of a novel directed draft graph to take advantage of the bidirectional, blockwise nature of dLLM generation. These draft graphs are calibrated offline to maximize acceptance rates and are dynamically pruned during inference for improved computational efficiency. We present a detailed formulation of Spiffy and demonstrate its ability to accelerate LLaDA, Dream, and SDAR models in combination with KV caching and threshold-based dynamic unmasking leading to up to $8.6\\times$ reduction in model inferences and $6.3\\times$ acceleration in token rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Original version uploaded on Sep 22, 2025. (v2): Extended Table 2 with additional analysis and referenced it in Sec 5.2. (v3): Added note to Sec 4.2 and Appendix A.2 specifying conditions for losslessness. (v4): Updated with the version accepted to ICML 2026 workshops"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18085","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:14Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-12T02:19:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16456","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16456","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16456","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zelei","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Zelei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GPO: Learning from Critical Steps to Improve LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-19T22:30:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:11:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T02:20:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:23:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:25:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:17:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in various domains, showing impressive potential on different tasks. Recently, reasoning LLMs have been proposed to improve the \\textit{reasoning} or \\textit{thinking} capabilities of LLMs to solve complex problems. Despite the promising results of reasoning LLMs, enhancing the multi-step reasoning capabilities of LLMs still remains a significant challenge. While existing optimization methods have advanced the LLM reasoning capabilities, they often treat reasoning trajectories as a whole, without considering the underlying critical steps within the trajectory. In this paper, we introduce \\textbf{G}uided \\textbf{P}ivotal \\textbf{O}ptimization (GPO), a novel fine-tuning strategy that dives into the reasoning process to enable more effective improvements. GPO first identifies the `critical step' within a reasoning trajectory - a point that the model must carefully proceed to succeed at the problem. We locate the critical step by estimating the advantage function. GPO then resets the policy to the critical step, samples the new rollout and prioritizes the learning process on those rollouts. This focus allows the model to learn more effectively from pivotal moments within the reasoning process to improve the reasoning performance. We demonstrate that GPO is a general strategy that can be integrated with various optimization methods to improve reasoning performance. Besides theoretical analysis, our experiments across challenging reasoning benchmarks show that GPO can consistently and significantly enhance the performance of existing optimization methods, showcasing its effectiveness and generalizability in improving LLM reasoning by concentrating on pivotal moments within the generation process."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16456","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:48:49Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:48:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:24:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sankeerth","familyName":"Durvasula","name":"Durvasula, Sankeerth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kavya","familyName":"Sreedhar","name":"Sreedhar, Kavya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zain","familyName":"Moustafa","name":"Moustafa, Zain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suraj","familyName":"Kothawade","name":"Kothawade, Suraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlei","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Tianlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish","familyName":"Gondimalla","name":"Gondimalla, Ashish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suvinay","familyName":"Subramanian","name":"Subramanian, Suvinay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narges","familyName":"Shahidi","name":"Shahidi, Narges","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nandita","familyName":"Vijaykumar","name":"Vijaykumar, Nandita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FG-Attn: Leveraging Fine-Grained Sparse Attention in Video Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T03:48:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:16:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T19:42:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:01:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using diffusion transformers for media generation may require evaluating attention over extremely long sequences, with attention layers accounting for the majority of generation latency. Exploiting sparsity in attention maps offers a promising opportunity to reduce this cost. In this work, we show that attention maps in diffusion transformers exhibit significant fine-grained sparsity in video generation models. Existing sparse attention methods, however, are too coarse-grained, leaving a large fraction of redundant computation unaddressed, or incur high overheads at finer granularity. We propose FG-Attn, a novel, low-overhead fine-grained sparse attention mechanism that skips score computations at the granularity of a MxN tile, where N\u0026gt;=1 and M\u0026gt;=16, and where each block is the result of query-key dot products between M queries and N keys. FG-Attn addresses the key challenge of hardware underutilization in sparse attention kernels on GPUs, without incurring the overheads of irregular memory access and redundant operations. FG-Attn can fully supersede existing sparse attention methods and extend block sparse attention methods to finer granularities on modern GPUs. At 70% sparsity, FG-Attn is up to 2.45X faster than the state-of-art FlashInfer, and reduces attention kernel time by 14.7% on average. FG-Attn speeds up end-to-end video generation times by up to 1.40X (1.18X on average) over Flash Attention 3."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:17Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:29:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haofeng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangfan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yangfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxin","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Yaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MVCL-DAF++: Enhancing Multimodal Intent Recognition via Prototype-Aware Contrastive Alignment and Coarse-to-Fine Dynamic Attention Fusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:38:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:15:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:50:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:24:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T06:20:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:27:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-08T05:25:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:22:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal intent recognition (MMIR) suffers from weak semantic grounding and poor robustness under noisy or rare-class conditions. We propose MVCL-DAF++, which extends MVCL-DAF with two key modules: (1) Prototype-aware contrastive alignment, aligning instances to class-level prototypes to enhance semantic consistency; and (2) Coarse-to-fine attention fusion, integrating global modality summaries with token-level features for hierarchical cross-modal interaction. On MIntRec and MIntRec2.0, MVCL-DAF++ achieves new state-of-the-art results, improving rare-class recognition by +1.05\\% and +4.18\\% WF1, respectively. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of prototype-guided learning and coarse-to-fine fusion for robust multimodal understanding. The source code is available at https://github.com/chr1s623/MVCL-DAF-PlusPlus."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:12:11Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:12:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:12:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nakul","familyName":"Garg","name":"Garg, Nakul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RadarSFD: Single-Frame Diffusion with Pretrained Priors for Radar Point Clouds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:49:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:50:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T22:18:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-03T00:14:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Millimeter-wave radar provides robust perception in fog, smoke, dust, and low light, making it attractive for size-, weight-, and power-constrained robotic platforms. Existing radar imaging methods typically rely on synthetic aperture or multi-frame aggregation to improve resolution, which is impractical for small aerial, inspection, or wearable systems. We present RadarSFD, a conditional latent diffusion framework that reconstructs dense LiDAR-like point clouds from a single radar frame without motion or SAR. Our approach transfers geometric priors from a pretrained monocular depth estimator into the diffusion backbone, anchors them to radar inputs via channel-wise latent concatenation, and regularizes outputs with a dual-space objective combining latent and pixel-space losses. On the RadarHD benchmark, RadarSFD achieves state-of-the-art performance against baseline models. Qualitative results show recovery of fine walls and narrow gaps, and experiments across new environments confirm strong generalization. Ablation studies highlight the importance of pretrained initialization, radar BEV conditioning, and the dual-space loss. Together, these results establish a practical single-frame, no-SAR mmWave radar pipeline for dense point cloud perception in compact robotic systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 2026 IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA 2026). Project page: https://phi-lab-rice.github.io/RadarSFD/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:26:51Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:26:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:10:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17531","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17531","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17531","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Holbach","name":"Holbach, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Bastian","name":"Bastian, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Scheichl","name":"Scheichl, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust spectral preconditioning for high-Péclet number convection-diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"65F10, 65N22, 65N55","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T08:52:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:20:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-02T08:11:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-03T00:40:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce a two-level hybrid restricted additive Schwarz (RAS) preconditioner for heterogeneous steady-state convection-diffusion equations at high Péclet numbers. Our construction builds on the multiscale spectral generalized finite element method (MS-GFEM), wherein the coarse space is spanned by locally optimal basis functions obtained from local generalized eigenproblems on operator-harmonic spaces. Extending the theory of Ma (2025) to convection-diffusion problems in conservation form, we establish exponential convergence of the MS-GFEM approximation with respect to the dimension of the local approximation space. Rewriting MS-GFEM as a RAS-type iteration, we show for coercive problems that this exponential convergence property is inherited by the RAS-type iterative method (at least in the continuous setting). Employed as a preconditioner within the generalized minimal residual method (GMRES), the resulting method requires only a few iterations for high accuracy even with low-dimensional coarse spaces.\n Through extensive numerical experiments on problems with high-contrast diffusion and non-divergence-free, rotating velocity fields, we demonstrate robustness with respect to the grid Péclet number and the number of subdomains (tested up to $10^5$ subdomains), while coarse-space dimensions remain small as grid Péclet numbers increase. By adapting the coarse space and oversampling size, we are able to achieve arbitrarily fast convergence of preconditioned GMRES. As an extension, for which we do not have theory yet, we show effectiveness of the method even for indefinite problems and in the vanishing-diffusion limit."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17531","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:14:12Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:14:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:10:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xulin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinhong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qinhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Mang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nenghai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Nenghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Anytime Retrieval: A Benchmark for Anytime Person Re-Identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T11:20:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:26:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-30T19:40:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:49:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In real applications, person re-identification (ReID) is expected to retrieve the target person at any time, including both daytime and nighttime, ranging from short-term to long-term. However, existing ReID tasks and datasets can not meet this requirement, as they are constrained by available time and only provide training and evaluation for specific scenarios. Therefore, we investigate a new task called Anytime Person Re-identification (AT-ReID), which aims to achieve effective retrieval in multiple scenarios based on variations in time. To address the AT-ReID problem, we collect the first large-scale dataset, AT-USTC, which contains 403k images of individuals wearing multiple clothes captured by RGB and IR cameras. Our data collection spans 21 months, and 270 volunteers were photographed on average 29.1 times across different dates or scenes, 4-15 times more than current datasets, providing conditions for follow-up investigations in AT-ReID. Further, to tackle the new challenge of multi-scenario retrieval, we propose a unified model named Uni-AT, which comprises a multi-scenario ReID (MS-ReID) framework for scenario-specific features learning, a Mixture-of-Attribute-Experts (MoAE) module to alleviate inter-scenario interference, and a Hierarchical Dynamic Weighting (HDW) strategy to ensure balanced training across all scenarios. Extensive experiments show that our model leads to satisfactory results and exhibits excellent generalization to all scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IJCAI 2025 (oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:53:01Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:53:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:52:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minsoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minsoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnav","familyName":"Kundu","name":"Kundu, Arnav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han-Byul","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Han-Byul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richa","familyName":"Dixit","name":"Dixit, Richa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minsik","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Minsik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EpiCache: Episodic KV Cache Management for Long-Term Conversation on Resource-Constrained Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T06:56:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:13:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T10:24:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:39:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-11T09:04:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:30:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T20:55:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:12:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern large language models (LLMs) extend context lengths to millions of tokens, enabling coherent, personalized responses grounded in long conversational history. However, the Key-Value (KV) cache grows linearly with the extended dialogue history, causing the model's memory footprint to quickly exceed device limits. While recent KV cache compression methods attempt to reduce memory usage, most apply cache eviction after processing the entire context, incurring unbounded peak memory usage. Additionally, query-dependent eviction narrows the cache semantics to a single query, leading to failure cases in multi-turn conversations. In this paper, we introduce EpiCache, a training-free KV cache management framework for long conversational question answering (LongConvQA) under fixed memory budgets. EpiCache bounds cache growth through block-wise prefill and preserves topic-relevant context via episodic KV compression, which clusters conversation history into coherent episodes and performs episode-specific KV cache eviction. Across three LongConvQA benchmarks (LongMemEval, Realtalk, and LoCoMo), EpiCache improves accuracy by up to 30%, achieves near full-cache accuracy under 4-6x compression, and reduces latency and peak memory by up to 2.4x and 3.7x, respectively."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:11:00Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:11:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:34:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17428","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17428","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17428","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyesung","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hyesung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seojune","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seojune","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beomseok","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Beomseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulhwa","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yulhwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jae-Joon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jae-Joon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QWHA: Quantization-Aware Walsh-Hadamard Adaptation for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning on Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:21:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:14:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T07:49:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:38:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T11:23:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-27T03:15:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:28:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The demand for efficient deployment of large language models (LLMs) has driven interest in quantization, which reduces inference cost, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT), which lowers training overhead. This motivated the development of quantization-aware PEFT to produce accurate yet efficient quantized models. In this setting, reducing quantization error prior to fine-tuning is crucial for achieving high model accuracy. However, existing methods that rely on low-rank adaptation suffer from limited representational capacity. Recent Fourier-related transform (FT)-based adapters offer greater representational power than low-rank adapters, but their direct integration into quantized models often results in ineffective error reduction and increased computational overhead. To overcome these limitations, we propose QWHA, a method that integrates FT-based adapters into quantized models by employing the Walsh-Hadamard Transform (WHT) as the transform kernel, together with a novel adapter initialization scheme incorporating adaptive parameter selection and value refinement. We demonstrate that QWHA effectively mitigates quantization errors while facilitating fine-tuning, and that its design substantially reduces computational cost. Experimental results show that QWHA consistently outperforms baselines in low-bit quantization accuracy and achieves significant training speedups over existing FT-based adapters. The code is available at https://github.com/vantaa89/qwha."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 9 figures, 14 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17428","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:11:46Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:11:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:45:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenghao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Shenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikai","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuangwu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuangwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When TableQA Meets Noise: A Dual Denoising Framework for Complex Questions and Large-scale Tables"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T12:25:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:28:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-18T16:40:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T02:23:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Table question answering (TableQA) is a fundamental task in natural language processing (NLP). The strong reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) have brought significant advances in this field. However, as real-world applications involve increasingly complex questions and larger tables, substantial noisy data is introduced, which severely degrades reasoning performance. To address this challenge, we focus on improving two core capabilities: Relevance Filtering, which identifies and retains information truly relevant to reasoning, and Table Pruning, which reduces table size while preserving essential content. Based on these principles, we propose EnoTab, a dual denoising framework for complex questions and large-scale tables. Specifically, we first perform Evidence-based Question Denoising by decomposing the question into minimal semantic units and filtering out those irrelevant to answer reasoning based on consistency and usability criteria. Then, we propose Evidence Tree-guided Table Denoising, which constructs an explicit and transparent table pruning path to remove irrelevant data step by step. At each pruning step, we observe the intermediate state of the table and apply a post-order node rollback mechanism to handle abnormal table states, ultimately producing a highly reliable sub-table for final answer reasoning. Finally, extensive experiments show that EnoTab achieves outstanding performance on TableQA tasks with complex questions and large-scale tables, confirming its effectiveness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 24 figures, accepted to ACL 2026 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:17:44Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:17:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:47:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mimo","familyName":"Shirasaka","name":"Shirasaka, Mimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristian C.","familyName":"Beltran-Hernandez","name":"Beltran-Hernandez, Cristian C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masashi","familyName":"Hamaya","name":"Hamaya, Masashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshitaka","familyName":"Ushiku","name":"Ushiku, Yoshitaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust and Resilient Soft Robotic Object Insertion with Compliance-Enabled Contact Formation and Failure Recovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T12:10:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:27:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-17T00:52:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T00:55:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Object insertion tasks are prone to failure under pose uncertainty and environmental variation, often requiring manual fine-tuning or controller retraining. We present a novel approach for robust and resilient object insertion using a passively compliant soft wrist that enables safe contact absorption through large deformations, without high-frequency control or force sensing. Our method structures insertion as compliance-enabled contact formations, sequential contact states that progressively constrain degrees of freedom, and integrates automated failure recovery strategies. Our key insight is that wrist compliance permits safe, repeated recovery attempts; hence, we refer to it as compliance-enabled failure recovery. We employ a pre-trained vision-language model (VLM) that assesses each skill execution from terminal poses and images, identifies failure modes, and proposes recovery actions by selecting skills and updating goals. In simulation, our method achieved an 83% success rate, recovering from failures induced by randomized conditions, including grasp misalignments up to 5 degrees, hole-pose errors up to 20 mm, fivefold increases in friction, and unseen square/rectangular pegs, and we further validated the approach on a real robot. Project page is available at https://omron-sinicx.github.io/compliance-enabled-failure-recovery/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:17:24Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:17:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:47:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17355","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17355","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17355","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ckristian","familyName":"Duran","name":"Duran, Ckristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanako","familyName":"Kimura","name":"Kimura, Nanako","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zolboo","familyName":"Byambadorj","name":"Byambadorj, Zolboo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Iizuka","name":"Iizuka, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CMOS Implementation of Field Programmable Spiking Neural Network for Hardware Reservoir Computing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T05:19:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-18T00:12:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/2634-4386/ae66b2","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing complexity and energy demands of large-scale neural networks, such as Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) and Large Language Models (LLMs), challenge their practical deployment in edge applications due to high power consumption, area requirements, and privacy concerns. Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs), particularly in analog implementations, offer a promising low-power alternative but suffer from noise sensitivity and connectivity limitations. This work presents a novel CMOS-implemented field-programmable neural network architecture for hardware reservoir computing. We propose a Leaky Integrate-and-Fire (LIF) neuron circuit with integrated voltage-controlled oscillators (VCOs) and programmable weighted interconnections via an on-chip FPGA framework, enabling arbitrary reservoir configurations. The system demonstrates effective implementation of the FORCE algorithm learning, linear and non-linear memory capacity benchmarks, and NARMA10 tasks, both in simulation and actual chip measurements. The neuron design achieves compact area utilization (around 540 NAND2-equivalent units) and low energy consumption (21.7 pJ/pulse) without requiring ADCs for information readout, making it ideal for system-on-chip integration of reservoir computing. This architecture paves the way for scalable, energy-efficient neuromorphic systems capable of performing real-time learning and inference with high configurability and digital interfacing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17355","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:10:01Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:10:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-18T02:13:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17189","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17189","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17189","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoran","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhuoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuolin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhuolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward a unified data-driven turbulence model through multi-objective learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T18:31:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:01:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-22T21:04:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:05:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T16:01:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-18T01:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Turbulence remains one of the last unresolved problems of classical physics and a major bottleneck to accurate flow prediction in climate, aerospace, and energy systems. Industrial simulations therefore rely on averaged representations of turbulence, which often struggle to predict flows governed by multiple interacting mechanisms. We present a unified, data-driven turbulence modeling framework designed to learn robustly from sparse, indirect observations across diverse flow regimes. The framework embeds physical consistency into a flexible, frame-invariant closure, automatically selects representative training cases based on similarity of flow-feature distributions, and learns a single, unified model through a multi-objective ensemble strategy that balances competing objectives across flows and quantities of interest. The resulting unified foundation model adapts seamlessly across regimes without manual intervention. It outperforms existing turbulence models across a broad spectrum of canonical flows and maintains improved performance in complex three-dimensional configurations of industrial relevance, including a gas turbine diffuser, a generic car, and a generic aircraft. When application-specific accuracy is required, the framework further enables specialist models through additive fine-tuning on targeted flow datasets. The results demonstrate the feasibility of a deployable and generalized turbulence modeling approach that unifies multiple flow mechanisms within a single architecture for a broad range of natural and industrial flows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in National Science Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17189","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:06:07Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:06:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-18T02:13:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederic","familyName":"Vatnsdal","name":"Vatnsdal, Frederic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Romina Garcia","familyName":"Camargo","name":"Camargo, Romina Garcia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurav","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Saurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Multi Agent Diffusion Policies for Coverage Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T21:34:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:05:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-06T14:57:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-07T00:53:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose MADP, a novel diffusion-model-based approach for collaboration in decentralized robot swarms. MADP leverages diffusion models to generate samples from complex and high-dimensional action distributions that capture the interdependencies between agents' actions. Each robot conditions policy sampling on a fused representation of its own observations and perceptual embeddings received from peers. To evaluate this approach, we task a team of holonomic robots piloted by MADP to address coverage control-a canonical multi agent navigation problem. The policy is trained via imitation learning from a clairvoyant expert on the coverage control problem, with the diffusion process parameterized by a spatial transformer architecture to enable decentralized inference. We evaluate the system under varying numbers, locations, and variances of importance density functions, capturing the robustness demands of real-world coverage tasks. Experiments demonstrate that our model inherits valuable properties from diffusion models, generalizing across agent densities and environments, and consistently outperforming state-of-the-art baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:07:26Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:07:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-07T02:18:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17677","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17677","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17677","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinlei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixi","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Ruixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulu","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yulu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjin","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jinjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhua","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Junhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EngiBench: A Benchmark for Evaluating Large Language Models on Engineering Problem Solving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T12:20:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:27:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-02T16:35:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:38:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown strong performance on mathematical reasoning under well-defined conditions. However, real-world engineering problems involve uncertainty, context, and open-ended settings that extend beyond symbolic computation. Existing benchmarks largely focus on well-defined or abstract reasoning and therefore fail to capture these complexities. We introduce EngiBench, a hierarchical benchmark designed to evaluate LLMs on solving engineering problems. It spans three levels of increasing difficulty (foundational knowledge retrieval, contextual reasoning, and open-ended modeling) and covers diverse engineering subfields. To facilitate a deeper understanding of model performance, we systematically rewrite each problem into three controlled variants (perturbed, knowledge-enhanced, and math abstraction), enabling us to separately evaluate the model's robustness, domain-specific knowledge, and mathematical reasoning abilities. Experimental results show clear performance stratification across difficulty levels: model accuracy declines with task complexity, degrades under minor perturbations, and remains substantially below human performance on high-level engineering tasks. These findings reveal that current LLMs still lack the high-level reasoning needed for real-world engineering, highlighting the need for future models with deeper and more reliable problem-solving capabilities. Our source code and data are available at https://github.com/AI4Engi/EngiBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17677","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:17:40Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:17:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16591","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16591","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16591","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengjie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengzhang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Mengzhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conghui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Conghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heterogeneous Adaptive Policy Optimization: Tailoring Optimization to Every Token's Nature"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T09:30:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:24:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T06:03:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:26:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using entropy as a measure of heterogeneity to guide optimization has emerged as a crucial research direction in Reinforcement Learning for LLMs. However, existing methods typically treat it as a discrete filter or post-hoc regulator rather than a core optimization driver. To fully leverage the potential of entropy and achieve fine-grained regulation, we introduce Heterogeneous Adaptive Policy Optimization (HAPO), a token-aware algorithm that continuously adapts optimization dynamics based on token-level entropy throughout the entire training process. Our algorithm includes four key components: (1) Adaptive Temperature Sampling that adjusts sampling temperature in real time, promoting exploration at high-entropy tokens. (2) Token-Level Group Average Advantage Estimation that estimates advantages at token level, accounting for sequence-length effects while preserving non-biased treatment.(3) Differential Advantage Redistribution that leverages entropy and importance ratios to adjust advantages for tokens with clear signals. (4) Asymmetric Adaptive Clipping that adynamically adjusts clipping boundaries based on token-level entropy. Through systematic investigation of entropy, we embed token-level treatment into every stage. Extensive experiments on mathematical reasoning, code, and logic tasks across multiple models demonstrate HAPO's consistent superiority over DAPO. Our code can be found in https://github.com/starriver030515/HAPO."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16591","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:59Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Ziheng","name":"Ziheng, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huacong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Huacong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjie","familyName":"Bi","name":"Bi, Mingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yipeng","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanying","familyName":"He","name":"He, Wanying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying Nian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ying Nian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Demetri","familyName":"Terzopoulos","name":"Terzopoulos, Demetri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangwei","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Fangwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why Are We Moral? An LLM-based Agent Simulation Approach to Study Moral Evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T12:43:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:29:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-27T16:56:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T02:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The evolution of morality presents a puzzle: natural selection should favor self-interest, yet humans developed moral systems promoting altruism. Traditional approaches must abstract away cognitive processes, leaving open how cognitive factors shape moral evolution. We introduce an LLM-based agent simulation framework that brings cognitive realism to this question: agents with varying moral dispositions perceive, remember, reason, and decide in a simulated prehistoric hunter-gatherer society. This enables us to manipulate factors that traditional models cannot represent -- such as moral type observability and communication bandwidth -- and to discover emergent cognitive mechanisms from agent interactions. Across 20 runs spanning four settings, we find that cooperation and mutual help are the central driver of evolutionary survival, with universal and reciprocal morality exhibiting the most stable outcomes across conditions while selfishness is strongly disfavoured. Beyond cooperation itself, we further identify cognition as a central mediator -- most clearly through a cost of moral judgment that shifts the winning moral type across settings, with a self-purging effect among selfish agents as an additional cognitive pattern. We validate robustness across multiple LLM backbones, architecture ablations, and prompt sensitivity analyses. This work establishes LLM-based simulation as a powerful new paradigm to complement traditional research in evolutionary biology and anthropology, opening new avenues for investigating the complexities of moral and social evolution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 Main Conference. 51 pages including appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:18:17Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:18:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-28T04:04:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16702","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16702","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16702","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kafeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiran","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Weiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Animalbooth: multimodal feature enhancement for animal subject personalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T14:09:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:32:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-27T07:06:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T01:21:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Personalized animal image generation is challenging due to rich appearance cues and large morphological variability. Existing approaches often exhibit feature misalignment across domains, which leads to identity drift. We present AnimalBooth, a framework that strengthens identity preservation with an Animal Net and an adaptive attention module, mitigating cross domain alignment errors. We further introduce a frequency controlled feature integration module that applies Discrete Cosine Transform filtering in the latent space to guide the diffusion process, enabling a coarse to fine progression from global structure to detailed texture. To advance research in this area, we curate AnimalBench, a high resolution dataset for animal personalization. Extensive experiments show that AnimalBooth consistently outperforms strong baselines on multiple benchmarks and improves both identity fidelity and perceptual quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16702","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:54:37Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:54:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-28T04:04:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16343","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16343","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16343","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chung-En Johnny","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chung-En Johnny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Jalaian","name":"Jalaian, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathaniel D.","familyName":"Bastian","name":"Bastian, Nathaniel D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Reasoning Agent: Robust Vision Systems in Remote Sensing via Inference-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-19T18:34:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T22:59:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:17:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building robust vision systems for high-stakes domains such as remote sensing requires stronger visual reasoning than what single-pass inference typically provides; yet, retraining large models is often computationally expensive and data intensive. We present Visual Reasoning Agent (VRA), a training-free agentic visual reasoning framework that orchestrates off-the-shelf large vision-language models (LVLMs) with a large reasoning model (LRM) through an iterative Think-Critique-Act loop for cross-model verification, self-critique, and recursive refinement. On the remote sensing benchmark VRSBench VQA dataset, VRA consistently outperforms multiple standalone LVLM baselines and achieves up to 40.67\\% improvement on challenging question types spanning both perception and reasoning tasks. In addition, integrating three LVLMs with VRA improves the overall accuracy of the standalone LVLMs from 52.8% to 78.8%, demonstrating the effectiveness of agentic reasoning with increased inference-time compute."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to MORS 2026 Artificial Intelligence Workshop Proceedings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16343","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:46:13Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:46:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:29:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Namyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Namyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai Tzu-iunn","familyName":"Ong","name":"Ong, Kai Tzu-iunn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeonjun","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Yeonjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minseok","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Minseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iiseo","familyName":"Jihn","name":"Jihn, Iiseo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gayoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Gayoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minju","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyoung","familyName":"Yeo","name":"Yeo, Jinyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PRINCIPLES: Synthetic Strategy Memory for Proactive Dialogue Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:53:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:36:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1164","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dialogue agents based on large language models (LLMs) have shown promising performance in proactive dialogue, which requires effective strategy planning. However, existing approaches to strategy planning for proactive dialogue face several limitations: limited strategy coverage, preference bias in planning, and reliance on costly additional training. To address these, we propose PRINCIPLES: a synthetic strategy memory for proactive dialogue agents. PRINCIPLES is derived through offline self-play simulations and serves as reusable knowledge that guides strategy planning during inference, eliminating the need for additional training and data annotation. We evaluate PRINCIPLES in both emotional support and persuasion domains, demonstrating consistent improvements over strong baselines. Furthermore, PRINCIPLES maintains its robustness across extended and more diverse evaluation settings. See our project page at https://huggingface.co/spaces/kimnamssya/Principles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:12:29Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:12:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:49:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16538","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16538","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16538","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubhashis Roy","familyName":"Dipta","name":"Dipta, Shubhashis Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tz-Ying","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Tz-Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subarna","familyName":"Tripathi","name":"Tripathi, Subarna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VC-Inspector: Advancing Reference-free Evaluation of Video Captions with Factual Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T05:04:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:17:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T08:32:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:41:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-19T03:58:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:59:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose VC-Inspector, a lightweight, open-source large multimodal model (LMM) for reference-free evaluation of video captions, with a focus on factual accuracy. Unlike existing metrics that suffer from limited context handling, weak factuality assessment, or reliance on proprietary services, VC-Inspector offers a reproducible and fact-aware alternative that aligns closely with human judgments. To enable robust training and interpretable evaluation, we introduce a systematic framework for generating captions with controllable factual errors, paired with graded quality scores and explanatory annotations. Experiments demonstrate that VC-Inspector achieves state-of-the-art correlation with human judgments, generalizing across diverse domains (e.g., VATEX-Eval, Flickr8K-Expert, and Flickr8K-CF benchmarks) and revealing the potential for caption improvement. Project page is available at https://dipta007.github.io/VC-Inspector"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16538","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:50:45Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:50:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:49:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun Seo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jun Seo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyemi","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyemi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woo Joo","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Woo Joo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjin","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Hongjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hochul","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hochul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hye Hyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hye Hyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-View Attention Multiple-Instance Learning Enhanced by LLM Reasoning for Cognitive Distortion Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T00:18:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:07:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-29T05:08:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:50:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-16T22:32:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:12:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cognitive distortions have been closely linked to mental health disorders, yet their automatic detection remains challenging due to contextual ambiguity, co-occurrence, and semantic overlap. We propose a novel framework that combines Large Language Models (LLMs) with a Multiple-Instance Learning (MIL) architecture to enhance interpretability and expression-level reasoning. Each utterance is decomposed into Emotion, Logic, and Behavior (ELB) components, which are processed by LLMs to infer multiple distortion instances, each with a predicted type, expression, and model-assigned salience score. These instances are integrated via a Multi-View Gated Attention mechanism for final classification. Experiments on Korean (KoACD) and English (Therapist QA) datasets demonstrate that incorporating ELB and LLM-inferred salience scores improves classification performance, especially for distortions with high interpretive ambiguity. Our results suggest a psychologically grounded and generalizable approach for fine-grained reasoning in mental health NLP. The dataset and implementation details are publicly accessible."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the main conference of ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:08:32Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:08:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:08:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16906","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16906","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16906","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gersena","familyName":"Banushi","name":"Banushi, Gersena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenichi","familyName":"Soga","name":"Soga, Kenichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transition Frequencies and Dynamic Amplification of Buried Lifelines: A Semi-Analytical Timoshenko Beam on Winkler Foundation Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Classical Physics (physics.class-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T03:49:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:05:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-13225-3_45","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Underground lifelines, such as pipelines and tunnels, are susceptible to ground vibrations from seismic events, traffic, and other dynamic sources. Accurate prediction of their response is essential for ensuring structural safety and operability. This study introduces a semi-analytical model for transverse vibration analysis of buried lifelines, formulated using the Timoshenko beam theory on elastic foundation. The closed-form analytical solutions revealed that the vibration spectrum comprises four parts, separated by three transition frequencies. At each transition, the oscillatory characteristics of the modes change as a function of the system properties, leading to marked variations in dynamic amplification. The model's validity is confirmed through case studies of buried steel pipelines of varying lengths and operating conditions, showing excellent agreement with finite element simulations. A subsequent parametric study quantifies the influence of key factors - including soil stiffness and system length - on dynamic performance. The proposed method provides a computationally efficient and physically transparent framework for capturing complex vibration phenomena beyond simplified travelling-wave approaches, offering valuable guidance for the design and resilience assessment of underground lifeline systems subjected to various dynamic loads."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at and accepted for publication in the official Proceedings of the 16th International Conference on Vibration Problems \u0026amp; 11th International Conference on Wave Mechanics and Vibrations (ICOVP \u0026amp; WMVC 2025), Lisbon, Portugal, September 2-5, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16906","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:59:22Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:59:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":440,"totalPages":18,"page":2},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-22T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-23T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%